{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgke15uVKjzWzP55CQm5Z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VmHC58hFuuHoLpHepxKcM2","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162033__x07393c/work/PLAN.md","offset":150,"limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6126,"cache_read_input_tokens":28481,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6126},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"feff64bd-df6d-45b2-b337-83604d3a2d73","uuid":"66aa3645-e927-4a1c-8731-46c7aa20bae7","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:20:44.772Z","request_id":"req_011Cgke15gc2bcqRUZoNEVqb","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01VmHC58hFuuHoLpHepxKcM2":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162033__x07393c/work/PLAN.md","offset":150,"limit":200}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VmHC58hFuuHoLpHepxKcM2","type":"tool_result","content":"150\t\n151\t**Flames.** The library's `fire-burst` is an explosion ring, not grass fire. Write your own strip. In `textureDef`, `size` is the whole strip, frames are equal slices, and each frame's `pixels` layer is placed with `at:[i*w,0]`:\n152\t```js\n153\ttextureDef({ id:'grass-flame', about:'Licking grass flame', tags:['fx'], size:[40,14], frames:4, palette:{a:'red',b:'orange',c:'sand',d:'white'},\n154\t  layers:[0,1,2,3].map(i => ({ at:[i*10,0], pixels:FRAMES[i], legend:{a:'a',b:'b',c:'c',d:'d'} })) });\n155\t// new SpriteBatch(spriteSheet(def), { capacity: 1800 }); batch.set(i, { frame }) every ~0.1 s\n156\t```\n157\tSprites of this sheet at 1.6 to 3.2 m look right. By night, one `ctx.lights.request({ color:0xff7a30, intensity:40, radius:60, flicker:'torch' })` over a fire front turns the grass deep red, which is very good.\n158\t\n159\t**People.**\n160\t- A `model()` with nested `pivot:true` groups named as `HERO_RIG`'s joints works with the hero's clips: `compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, { ...HERO_RIG, spine: undefined }, build.object)`.\n161\t- `new Ragdoll(physics, object, HERO_RAGDOLL)` also works on it. It costs about 15 draws per person.\n162\t- Use this soldier as your starting point (as written it passes `checkModel` with no problems or warnings; shoulders about 1.12 m, head top about 1.74 m, faces +Z):\n163\t```js\n164\tconst limb = (name, len, w, c, kids = [], at = [0,0,0]) => ({ group:[{ box:[w,len,w], at:[0,-len/2,0], c }, ...kids], at, pivot:true, name });\n165\tconst foot = (name) => ({ group:[{ box:[0.12,0.1,0.26], at:[0,-0.05,0.05], c:'boot' }], at:[0,-0.22,0], pivot:true, name });\n166\texport const soldier = model({ id:'soldier', about:'Soldier on the hero rig', tags:['actor'], floats:true, collider:'none',\n167\t  palette:{ cloth:'slate', dark:'night', skin:'plum', boot:'ink', beret:'navy', strap:'white' },\n168\t  parts:[{ group:[\n169\t    { box:[0.34,0.2,0.2], at:[0,0.06,0], c:'dark' },\n170\t    { group:[\n171\t      { box:[0.38,0.5,0.22], at:[0,0.25,0], c:'cloth' },\n172\t      { box:[0.06,0.56,0.24], at:[0.1,0.25,0], rot:[0,0,28], c:'strap' },\n173\t      { group:[{ box:[0.2,0.22,0.2], at:[0,0.11,0], c:'skin' }, { box:[0.24,0.07,0.24], at:[0.01,0.25,0], rot:[0,0,-8], c:'beret' }], at:[0,0.52,0], pivot:true, name:'Head' },\n174\t      limb('ArmR',0.25,0.11,'cloth',[limb('ForearmR',0.16,0.09,'cloth',[limb('HandR',0.08,0.08,'skin',[],[0,-0.16,0])],[0,-0.25,0])],[-0.25,0.47,0]),\n175\t      limb('ArmL',0.25,0.11,'cloth',[limb('ForearmL',0.16,0.09,'cloth',[limb('HandL',0.08,0.08,'skin',[],[0,-0.16,0])],[0,-0.25,0])],[0.25,0.47,0]),\n176\t    ], at:[0,0.04,0], pivot:true, name:'Torso' },\n177\t    limb('LegR',0.3,0.13,'dark',[limb('ShinR',0.22,0.11,'dark',[foot('FootR')],[0,-0.3,0])],[-0.14,0,0]),\n178\t    limb('LegL',0.3,0.13,'dark',[limb('ShinL',0.22,0.11,'dark',[foot('FootL')],[0,-0.3,0])],[0.14,0,0]),\n179\t  ], at:[0,0.62,0], pivot:true, name:'Pelvis' }] });\n180\t```\n181\t\n182\t**Custom rigs work too,** with `p` offsets on any joint. For first-person hands, use a rig like `{ joints:['Root','Weapon','Mag','Bolt','ArmR','HandR','ArmL','HandL'], root:'Root', mirror:{}, soles:[], trace:[], limits:{}, fps:30 }`, a model with those pivot names, `compileClips`, and a `THREE.AnimationMixer`. A key looks like `[6, { Weapon:{ r:[0,0,-35], p:[-.05,.06,.05] } }, 'out']`.\n183\t\n184\t**Checks.** Use plain distance tests in `fixedUpdate` for sites and pickups: the engine triggers' `tag:'character'` belongs to the platformer hero.\n185\t\n186\t**Movement.** `CharacterController` stays grounded on the heightfield and is right for a grounded soldier. `PlatformerCharacter` (triple jumps, ground pounds) is not. `speed` is set in the constructor and read as a plain field; `setInput(move, jump, jumpHeld)` takes a move vector of length up to 1.\n187\t\n188\t**Vehicles.** `new Vehicle(physics, { size:[0.95,0.45,2.2], mass:12, power:160, topSpeed:26, wheelRadius:0.42, suspension:0.45, stiffness:30, grip:2.6, at, yaw })` reaches 22 m/s in 3 s on the terrain and stays upright. The defaults only reach 7 to 11 m/s on the same ground.\n189\t\n190\t**Navigation.** `new NavGrid(640, 640, { cell:2, origin:[-640,0,-640] })`: a long `path()` took 26 ms, a short one 2 ms, and one `flowTo()` 114 ms. So use `path` with a budget (at most 2 per fixed step, queued) and never use flow fields here.\n191\t\n192\t**HUD.** 480×270 art pixels at 16:9. Font is 5×7 with a 6 px advance, drawn uppercase. It has exactly: A-Z, 0-9, space and ``. , : ; ! ? ' \" - + = / ( ) [ ] { } % _ # & * @ < > | \\ ~ ^ $ ` ° ·`` plus curly quotes. There is **no em dash or ellipsis**, so write `-` and `...`; unknown characters draw as `?`. Scale 1 is about 28 px tall on a 1080p screen, which I judged readable as the smallest size. `hud.width` follows the screen's aspect, so anchor everything. `hud.text` doesn't wrap: write your own wrapper.\n193\t\n194\t**Audio.**\n195\t- Default volumes: master 0.8, sfx 1, music 0.6, persisted by the engine. `resetVolumes()` returns to those, so implement your own reset.\n196\t- `renderSound(def)` is pure and works in Node.\n197\t- `audio.play(defOrName, { pitch, volume, pan })` and `audio.loop(...)` with `voice.set({ volume, pan, muffle, pitch })` cover everything.\n198\t- No audio files are needed, and none are allowed.\n199\t\n200\t**Tests in Node.**\n201\t- `Engine.headless(createGame(), { camera:{ preset:'first' }, viewport:{ width:1920, height:1080, dpr:1 } })` runs at far faster than real time (300 steps took about 60 ms on a bare scene).\n202\t- A pad works: `globalThis.navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad]`, with `pad = { connected:true, mapping:'standard', axes:[0,0,0,0], buttons:[...{pressed,value}] }`.\n203\t- Mouse works: `input.setPointer(x, y, down)`, read back through `input.buttonEvents`; `input.addPointer(dx, dy, wheel)` adds movement.\n204\t- `spriteSheet`, `textureMap`, `buildModel` and the HUD all work in Node. Only `drawSheet` needs a canvas: don't use it.\n205\t\n206\t**Hub.** I ran the real harness against an engine-weight shared-world page, with `card:true` and with the `LOOK` from section 6 for the two-player runs. All four scenarios the hub uses pass in 12 to 17 s, with `AIHub.ready()` called about 3 s after load (one straggler took 6.6 s):\n207\t- `players=2&auto=1`\n208\t- `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\n209\t- the same four-player settings with `&scenario=host-leave`\n210\t- `players=2&auto=1&scenario=server`\n211\t\n212\tA software-rendered copy only draws about 2 frames a second there, so **the net tick and the card's Enter key must not depend on the render loop** (section 19). For the server copy, `await Engine.headless(game, {})` in the browser page plus `setInterval(() => e.step(6, 1/60), 100)` works and needs no GPU.\n213\t\n214\t**Same-computer link.** `BroadcastChannel` works between two `file://` pages of one browser profile (verified).\n215\t\n216\t**A local server, when you need one.** `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` buffers its \"port\" line when its stdout is a pipe, so a script waiting for it hangs forever. Run it as `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, or print the port some other way.\n217\t\n218\t**Software rendering runs at about 1 fps here.** That is expected. Treat the performance budget as unlimited: add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights and shadows as a scene needs. FPS targets and optimization are out of scope and handled later. Never judge the game by real time in a headless browser. Drive tests with `e.step(n)` and read state. For a real picture, set `e.manual = false`, wait 10 s or more, and take a Playwright `page.screenshot`. That produced correct frames for me with the flags in section 24.\n219\t\n220\t---\n221\t\n222\t## 4. Architecture\n223\t\n224\tUnzip the kit **into the dev folder root** so `pixel-engine.js` and `check.mjs` sit at its top. The kit's tools find the engine by looking upward, so `node check.mjs --headless src/game.js` and `node check.mjs --sheets src/art/models.js` then just work. Never edit `pixel-engine.js`. Replace the kit's `game.js` and `index.html` with yours, and delete its `examples/` and `packs/` when you are done with them: the game loads no packs, so the credits and the bundle stay simple.\n225\t\n226\t```\n227\tpixel-engine.js check.mjs GUIDE.md API.md ...   the kit, as shipped\n228\tindex.html                the dev page: same <head> order as the bundle (section 23), loads src/main.js\n229\tvendor/session.js         AI Hub client, unmodified (downloaded from the hub's repository)\n230\tvendor/hub/               harness.html bridge.js round.js (tests only, never bundled)\n231\ttools/build.mjs           esbuild + inline: writes the one HTML file your brief names\n232\tsrc/main.js               browser boot only: hub connect, touch check, Engine.start\n233\tsrc/game.js               export createGame(options); the Game hooks; the mode machine; no DOM at import\n234\tsrc/core/                 actions.js look.js tuning.js save.js rng.js state.js events.js\n235\tsrc/world/                terrain.js roads.js sites.js grass.js sky.js fire.js wind.js map-texture.js\n236\tsrc/actors/               player.js soldier.js squad.js buddy.js civilian.js fauna.js rig.js\n237\tsrc/combat/               weapons.js projectiles.js damage.js viewmodel.js\n238\tsrc/body/                 health.js fever.js surgery.js\n239\tsrc/vehicles/             vehicle.js driving.js repair.js train.js\n240\tsrc/missions/             runner.js steps.js scripts.js ledger.js\n241\tsrc/ui/                   hud.js menu.js screens/*.js glyphs.js prompts.js dialogue.js pixfont.js touchgate.js about.js\n242\tsrc/dev/                  devmode.js sliders.js overlays.js\n243\tsrc/attract/              director.js beats.js\n244\tsrc/net/                  session.js hubSession.js linkSession.js soloSession.js round.js host.js client.js card.js\n245\tsrc/audio/                sfx.js music.js voice.js ambience.js\n246\tsrc/art/                  models.js textures.js viewmodels.js clips.js   (data only: exports for check.mjs --sheets)\n247\tsrc/content/              title.js weapons.js sites.js roads.js missions.js dialogue.js notes.js about.js tuning-defaults.js\n248\ttests/                    run.mjs lib/ suites/\n249\t```\n250\t\n251\t**Patterns to follow:**\n252\t- **One Game object, one Engine.** `createGame()` builds the world once. Title, attract, campaign and round are modes on it (`game.mode`). Don't use `engine.loadGame` between them: it would throw away the terrain. For New Game and Attract use `world.reset()`, which clears fire, sites and actors and repaints the base colours.\n253\t- **Data over code.** All content lives in `src/content/` and `src/art/` as plain data. Adding a weapon, site, mission, line of dialogue or model must not need a code change. This is what lets the rest of the game be built later \"with normal designer tools\": tables, the sheets tool and dev mode's placement readout. Keep a one-page `DESIGNERS.md` in the dev folder saying how to add each.\n254\t- **Actions, not keys.** `src/core/actions.js` maps an action to its pad code and its keyboard/mouse codes (section 16). All gameplay asks for actions. In `fixedUpdate` use `input.consumeAny(codes)`; in `update` use `wasPressed`.\n255\t- **Gameplay in `fixedUpdate`, presentation in `update`.** All randomness comes from `ctx.random.fork(name)`, never `Math.random`. Cosmetic forks are made in `setup`.\n256\t- **`TUNING`.** Every balance number lives in `src/content/tuning-defaults.js`, copied to a live `TUNING` object. Systems read `TUNING.x` at the moment of use and never cache it, so sliders act instantly.\n257\t- **`game.state()`.** One plain JSON snapshot for every test: `{ mode, screen, focus, device, glyphSet, notice, attract, mission, step, hp, stones, tablets, syrettes, weapon:{id,cond,jammed,mag}, fever, pos, yaw, vehicle, hour, wind, fire:{burning,burnt}, prompts:[...], subtitle, buddies, ledger }`. Expose it in `ready(ctx)` as `window.__GAME__ = game` when `window` exists. Give `status()` a useful one-liner and `fingerprint()` a hash of hp, stones, weapon state, fire counts and actor positions.\n258\t- **Sim and view apart.** A soldier is data plus an optional view; a fire cell is data plus sprites. In a round, a client's soldiers are puppets with no brain and no collider, driven by snapshots. Write them this way from the first day, because retrofitting it is the expensive part of section 19.\n259\t- **Simulation bubble.** Sites within 260 m of the player are live; others are counters. Vehicles on patrol are simulated within 400 m. This keeps the code simple and headless tests fast.\n260\t- **Guard the page.** Any code that touches `window`, `document`, `navigator` or `localStorage` checks that it exists. `src/game.js` must import cleanly in Node.\n261\t- **Issues log.** Keep `tests/ISSUES.md`: one line per bug testing catches, marked critical or not. Your brief asks how many there were at the end.\n262\t\n263\t**Working rules from your brief:** keep every file inside your own folder (not `/tmp`, not a parent). Don't read other agents' folders. Other agents share this machine, so keep to one or two browsers at once and close them. Don't run `playwright install`; use `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder. `uv run --with pillow ...` is the way to get Python packages. Stop only processes you started, by PID, and never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`. \"Target closed\" or \"page crashed\" means memory ran out: run fewer browsers rather than retrying. If you serve anything, confirm the page's title is your game before trusting a result.\n264\t\n265\t---\n266\t\n267\t## 5. World\n268\t\n269\tThe whole game happens on one continuous 1280 × 1280 m terrain (x and z from -640 to 640, +x east, -z north, 512 × 512 cells at 2.5 m). Heights have a +40 offset.\n270\t\n271\t**Height function,** built up in this order:\n272\t1. `40 + 14*fbm(x*.0032, z*.0032) + 2.5*fbm(x*.02, z*.02)`.\n273\t2. The north ridge: up to +42 m with ridged noise around (-130, -520), radius 260.\n274\t3. The west escarpment: rising +30 m for x < -520, cut by the Pass at (-590, -430).\n275\t4. The salt pan: flattened to y = 36 around (200, 470), radius 150.\n276\t5. The mine: a terraced pit at (150, -300), radius 70, 24 m deep.\n277\t6. The river: a channel along its polyline, 14 m wide and 3 m below the banks.\n278\t7. The stamps: flatten around each site (radial falloff) and along each road (5 m wide, 3 m shoulder).\n279\t\n280\tGenerate once in `setup`; don't run erosion.\n281\t\n282\t**Ground colour:** per cell after generating, in palette colours: sand and orange for grass, red for dirt and roads, plum for rock (slope), mist and white for the salt pan, green for riverbank and thorn woodland, teal near water. Keep a copy of the base colours for scars and regrowth.\n283\t\n284\t**Water:** one `WaterSurface` strip per river segment, with river depth 1 m everywhere. Wading halves your speed and stops sprinting. Vehicles cross only at the two bridges and the ford.\n285\t\n286\t**Sites.** All in `src/content/sites.js` as data. Tune positions by eye; the arc of the East Siding, the Cut, the Long Field and the Pass is the one thing that must stay (it's the plot).\n287\t\n288\t| Site | At (x, z) | What it is |\n289\t| --- | --- | --- |\n290\t| Mile Forty | (0, 40) | Truce town, no-fire zone radius 70 until mission M7: inside it your gun is slung and won't fire, and nobody shoots. Station, clinic, Tibor's shed, bar, market, notice board |\n291\t| Pump Shed | (-120, 150) | First safehouse, empty |\n292\t| Ranger Post | (40, -170) | Concord post with a cage, becomes a safehouse after M2 |\n293\t| Water Tower | (470, -180) | Safehouse, guarded by 6 until cleared |\n294\t| Fort Clement | (-430, -180) | Concord HQ: colonial stone walls (library `stone-wall`, `battlement`), parade ground, Vance's office |\n295\t| The Long Field | (-260, -300) | Concord farmland, 180 × 120 m of tall dry crop, ammo bunker |\n296\t| The Granary | (400, 280) | Harvest HQ: silos, tin sheds, Ansa's schoolroom |\n297\t| East Siding | (430, -60) | Harvest fuel depot on the railway, 3 tanks |\n298\t| Three Wells | (-260, 240) | Village, 12 civilians, wells, cistern |\n299\t| Pump Station | (-330, 395) | On the river, feeds Three Wells |\n300\t| Salt Pan | (200, 470) | Payroll road across white flats |\n301\t| The Cut | (150, -300) | Open-pit mine: headframe, sluice house, spoil heaps |\n302\t| Observatory | (-130, -520) | Harrow's trig station on the ridge |\n303\t| The Pass | (-590, -430) | Mined border pass; the end |\n304\t\n305\t**Checkpoints** (posts of 4 to 6 soldiers, sandbags, a boom gate, sometimes a technical): P1 (90, -80) north road · P2 (-200, 30) west road · P3 (170, 90) east road · P4 (-320, -90) fort road · P5 (280, -210) mine road · P6 (300, 390) salt road. A cleared post re-mans after `TUNING.postRemanHours` (24 game hours); the source's instant respawn is one of its known annoyances, so we don't copy it.\n306\t\n307\t**Roads** (polylines in `src/content/roads.js`, painted red, flattened):\n308\t- Mile Forty to P1, Ranger Post, P5, the Cut.\n309\t- Mile Forty to P2, Three Wells, Pump Station.\n310\t- P2 to P4, Fort Clement, the Long Field.\n311\t- Mile Forty to P3 to the bridge at (250, 80), then forking to the Granary and to East Siding, then on to the Water Tower.\n312\t- The Granary to P6 to the Salt Pan.\n313\t- The back road: Mile Forty over the bridge at (120, 230) to P6.\n314\t- The Long Field by a track to the Pass.\n315\t- The Observatory by a switchback track from the Cut.\n316\t\n317\t**The railway:** a polyline from (-640, 110) through Mile Forty (0, 70) to East Siding (430, -60) and on to (640, -110), with a spur north to the Cut. Rails are `mine-rail` pieces or two long boxes, with their own bridge where they cross the river near (270, -5). Four **rail halts** (West Cutting, Mile Forty, East Siding, Cut Spur).\n318\t\n319\t**The Mercy (river):** (600,-640) → (420,-320) → (300,-120) → (250,80) → (120,230) → (-60,300) → (-240,380) → (-420,430) → (-640,470). Road bridges at (250, 80) and (120, 230); a ford at (-60, 300), which a track from Mile Forty uses.\n320\t\n321\t**Plants and scatter** (seeded, stored per cell for the fire):\n322\t- About 400 trees (`acacia` shapes and a fat bottle tree), denser in the east woodland. Each has a `addStaticCylinder` trunk.\n323\t- Thorn bushes (library `bush` recoloured).\n324\t- Termite mounds (own model).\n325\t- Boulders and `rock` on the ridge. Library `dead-tree`.\n326\t- Reeds on the river.\n327\t\n328\t**Grass:** `GrassField` chunks of 48 × 48 m with about 7,000 blades, `mask` from the fuel map, `ground` from `heightAt`. Build a chunk the first time it comes within 170 m of the camera, hide it beyond 200 m, dispose beyond 320 m. On build, consult the fire grid (burnt cells get no blades).\n329\t\n330\t**Buildings** are `model()` defs with `collider:'parts'` so doorways stay open. Single storey with open doorways; no doors, no ladders. Watchtowers carry a soldier the player can shoot but not climb to.\n331\t\n332\t**Fauna:** gazelle herds in open grass (a small quadruped on its own rig with a keyed run cycle, fleeing gunshots and fire) and vultures circling burnt or corpse-heavy sites (`Boids` with a tiny flapping model). They are atmosphere and can't hurt you.\n333\t\n334\t**Civilians:** walk between points in Mile Forty and Three Wells, using the hero clips. Flee on gunfire. They cannot be killed by the player's bullets (hits are ignored and they flee), and fire makes them flee; the Ledger counts displacement, not bodies.\n335\t\n336\t---\n337\t\n338\t## 6. Fire, wind, time, weather\n339\t\n340\t**The fire grid** is the game's signature system, so write it first and test it hardest.\n341\t\n342\t- 256 × 256 cells at 5 m (`src/world/fire.js`). Arrays: `fuel` (0 none, 1 grass, 2 scrub, 3 tree, 4 building), `state` (0 fresh, 1 burning, 2 burnt, 3 regrowing), `burnEnd` and `burntAt`. Road, water, rock, salt and dirt have no fuel.\n343\t- Tick at 4 Hz in `fixedUpdate` (every 15 steps), using `ctx.random.fork('fire')`. Keep a list of burning cells; never scan the grid.\n344\t- Each burning cell tries its 8 neighbours with `p = 0.02 * fuelFactor * windFactor * slopeFactor * rainFactor * TUNING.fireSpread`, divided by 1.41 for diagonals. I calibrated 0.02 by simulating this grid: on flat grass the head, flank and back of the fire move at 1.9, 1.9, 1.9 m/s with no wind, 3.9, 2.4, 0.35 at 5 m/s, and 4.9, 2.9, 0.4 at 8 m/s. Larger bases run away quickly (0.08 gives about 10 m/s and burns upwind too).\n345\t  - `windFactor = clamp(1 + TUNING.windEffect * windSpeed/5 * 1.6 * cos(angle between neighbour direction and wind), 0.15, 3.5)`\n346\t  - `slopeFactor` = 1.4 uphill, 0.7 downhill. `rainFactor` = 0.15 while raining. `fuelFactor`: grass 1, scrub 0.8, tree 0.6, building 0.4.\n347\t- Strong wind (above 6 m/s) gives an ember jump: small chance to light a fuel cell 2 to 3 cells downwind, which is how fire crosses a road.\n348\t- Burn time: grass 8 to 14 s, scrub 12 to 18 s, tree and building 30 to 40 s.\n349\t- **A test must measure the head of the fire at wind 5 m/s and require 2 to 5 m/s,** with flanks clearly slower, so a sprinting or running player can escape and a walking one (3.6 m/s) struggles."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"feff64bd-df6d-45b2-b337-83604d3a2d73","uuid":"44e6dc99-3fff-4559-8a3b-2bb3a440118c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:20:44.787Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162033__x07393c/work/PLAN.md","content":"\n**Flames.** The library's `fire-burst` is an explosion ring, not grass fire. Write your own strip. In `textureDef`, `size` is the whole strip, frames are equal slices, and each frame's `pixels` layer is placed with `at:[i*w,0]`:\n```js\ntextureDef({ id:'grass-flame', about:'Licking grass flame', tags:['fx'], size:[40,14], frames:4, palette:{a:'red',b:'orange',c:'sand',d:'white'},\n  layers:[0,1,2,3].map(i => ({ at:[i*10,0], pixels:FRAMES[i], legend:{a:'a',b:'b',c:'c',d:'d'} })) });\n// new SpriteBatch(spriteSheet(def), { capacity: 1800 }); batch.set(i, { frame }) every ~0.1 s\n```\nSprites of this sheet at 1.6 to 3.2 m look right. By night, one `ctx.lights.request({ color:0xff7a30, intensity:40, radius:60, flicker:'torch' })` over a fire front turns the grass deep red, which is very good.\n\n**People.**\n- A `model()` with nested `pivot:true` groups named as `HERO_RIG`'s joints works with the hero's clips: `compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, { ...HERO_RIG, spine: undefined }, build.object)`.\n- `new Ragdoll(physics, object, HERO_RAGDOLL)` also works on it. It costs about 15 draws per person.\n- Use this soldier as your starting point (as written it passes `checkModel` with no problems or warnings; shoulders about 1.12 m, head top about 1.74 m, faces +Z):\n```js\nconst limb = (name, len, w, c, kids = [], at = [0,0,0]) => ({ group:[{ box:[w,len,w], at:[0,-len/2,0], c }, ...kids], at, pivot:true, name });\nconst foot = (name) => ({ group:[{ box:[0.12,0.1,0.26], at:[0,-0.05,0.05], c:'boot' }], at:[0,-0.22,0], pivot:true, name });\nexport const soldier = model({ id:'soldier', about:'Soldier on the hero rig', tags:['actor'], floats:true, collider:'none',\n  palette:{ cloth:'slate', dark:'night', skin:'plum', boot:'ink', beret:'navy', strap:'white' },\n  parts:[{ group:[\n    { box:[0.34,0.2,0.2], at:[0,0.06,0], c:'dark' },\n    { group:[\n      { box:[0.38,0.5,0.22], at:[0,0.25,0], c:'cloth' },\n      { box:[0.06,0.56,0.24], at:[0.1,0.25,0], rot:[0,0,28], c:'strap' },\n      { group:[{ box:[0.2,0.22,0.2], at:[0,0.11,0], c:'skin' }, { box:[0.24,0.07,0.24], at:[0.01,0.25,0], rot:[0,0,-8], c:'beret' }], at:[0,0.52,0], pivot:true, name:'Head' },\n      limb('ArmR',0.25,0.11,'cloth',[limb('ForearmR',0.16,0.09,'cloth',[limb('HandR',0.08,0.08,'skin',[],[0,-0.16,0])],[0,-0.25,0])],[-0.25,0.47,0]),\n      limb('ArmL',0.25,0.11,'cloth',[limb('ForearmL',0.16,0.09,'cloth',[limb('HandL',0.08,0.08,'skin',[],[0,-0.16,0])],[0,-0.25,0])],[0.25,0.47,0]),\n    ], at:[0,0.04,0], pivot:true, name:'Torso' },\n    limb('LegR',0.3,0.13,'dark',[limb('ShinR',0.22,0.11,'dark',[foot('FootR')],[0,-0.3,0])],[-0.14,0,0]),\n    limb('LegL',0.3,0.13,'dark',[limb('ShinL',0.22,0.11,'dark',[foot('FootL')],[0,-0.3,0])],[0.14,0,0]),\n  ], at:[0,0.62,0], pivot:true, name:'Pelvis' }] });\n```\n\n**Custom rigs work too,** with `p` offsets on any joint. For first-person hands, use a rig like `{ joints:['Root','Weapon','Mag','Bolt','ArmR','HandR','ArmL','HandL'], root:'Root', mirror:{}, soles:[], trace:[], limits:{}, fps:30 }`, a model with those pivot names, `compileClips`, and a `THREE.AnimationMixer`. A key looks like `[6, { Weapon:{ r:[0,0,-35], p:[-.05,.06,.05] } }, 'out']`.\n\n**Checks.** Use plain distance tests in `fixedUpdate` for sites and pickups: the engine triggers' `tag:'character'` belongs to the platformer hero.\n\n**Movement.** `CharacterController` stays grounded on the heightfield and is right for a grounded soldier. `PlatformerCharacter` (triple jumps, ground pounds) is not. `speed` is set in the constructor and read as a plain field; `setInput(move, jump, jumpHeld)` takes a move vector of length up to 1.\n\n**Vehicles.** `new Vehicle(physics, { size:[0.95,0.45,2.2], mass:12, power:160, topSpeed:26, wheelRadius:0.42, suspension:0.45, stiffness:30, grip:2.6, at, yaw })` reaches 22 m/s in 3 s on the terrain and stays upright. The defaults only reach 7 to 11 m/s on the same ground.\n\n**Navigation.** `new NavGrid(640, 640, { cell:2, origin:[-640,0,-640] })`: a long `path()` took 26 ms, a short one 2 ms, and one `flowTo()` 114 ms. So use `path` with a budget (at most 2 per fixed step, queued) and never use flow fields here.\n\n**HUD.** 480×270 art pixels at 16:9. Font is 5×7 with a 6 px advance, drawn uppercase. It has exactly: A-Z, 0-9, space and ``. , : ; ! ? ' \" - + = / ( ) [ ] { } % _ # & * @ < > | \\ ~ ^ $ ` ° ·`` plus curly quotes. There is **no em dash or ellipsis**, so write `-` and `...`; unknown characters draw as `?`. Scale 1 is about 28 px tall on a 1080p screen, which I judged readable as the smallest size. `hud.width` follows the screen's aspect, so anchor everything. `hud.text` doesn't wrap: write your own wrapper.\n\n**Audio.**\n- Default volumes: master 0.8, sfx 1, music 0.6, persisted by the engine. `resetVolumes()` returns to those, so implement your own reset.\n- `renderSound(def)` is pure and works in Node.\n- `audio.play(defOrName, { pitch, volume, pan })` and `audio.loop(...)` with `voice.set({ volume, pan, muffle, pitch })` cover everything.\n- No audio files are needed, and none are allowed.\n\n**Tests in Node.**\n- `Engine.headless(createGame(), { camera:{ preset:'first' }, viewport:{ width:1920, height:1080, dpr:1 } })` runs at far faster than real time (300 steps took about 60 ms on a bare scene).\n- A pad works: `globalThis.navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad]`, with `pad = { connected:true, mapping:'standard', axes:[0,0,0,0], buttons:[...{pressed,value}] }`.\n- Mouse works: `input.setPointer(x, y, down)`, read back through `input.buttonEvents`; `input.addPointer(dx, dy, wheel)` adds movement.\n- `spriteSheet`, `textureMap`, `buildModel` and the HUD all work in Node. Only `drawSheet` needs a canvas: don't use it.\n\n**Hub.** I ran the real harness against an engine-weight shared-world page, with `card:true` and with the `LOOK` from section 6 for the two-player runs. All four scenarios the hub uses pass in 12 to 17 s, with `AIHub.ready()` called about 3 s after load (one straggler took 6.6 s):\n- `players=2&auto=1`\n- `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\n- the same four-player settings with `&scenario=host-leave`\n- `players=2&auto=1&scenario=server`\n\nA software-rendered copy only draws about 2 frames a second there, so **the net tick and the card's Enter key must not depend on the render loop** (section 19). For the server copy, `await Engine.headless(game, {})` in the browser page plus `setInterval(() => e.step(6, 1/60), 100)` works and needs no GPU.\n\n**Same-computer link.** `BroadcastChannel` works between two `file://` pages of one browser profile (verified).\n\n**A local server, when you need one.** `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` buffers its \"port\" line when its stdout is a pipe, so a script waiting for it hangs forever. Run it as `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, or print the port some other way.\n\n**Software rendering runs at about 1 fps here.** That is expected. Treat the performance budget as unlimited: add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights and shadows as a scene needs. FPS targets and optimization are out of scope and handled later. Never judge the game by real time in a headless browser. Drive tests with `e.step(n)` and read state. For a real picture, set `e.manual = false`, wait 10 s or more, and take a Playwright `page.screenshot`. That produced correct frames for me with the flags in section 24.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Architecture\n\nUnzip the kit **into the dev folder root** so `pixel-engine.js` and `check.mjs` sit at its top. The kit's tools find the engine by looking upward, so `node check.mjs --headless src/game.js` and `node check.mjs --sheets src/art/models.js` then just work. Never edit `pixel-engine.js`. Replace the kit's `game.js` and `index.html` with yours, and delete its `examples/` and `packs/` when you are done with them: the game loads no packs, so the credits and the bundle stay simple.\n\n```\npixel-engine.js check.mjs GUIDE.md API.md ...   the kit, as shipped\nindex.html                the dev page: same <head> order as the bundle (section 23), loads src/main.js\nvendor/session.js         AI Hub client, unmodified (downloaded from the hub's repository)\nvendor/hub/               harness.html bridge.js round.js (tests only, never bundled)\ntools/build.mjs           esbuild + inline: writes the one HTML file your brief names\nsrc/main.js               browser boot only: hub connect, touch check, Engine.start\nsrc/game.js               export createGame(options); the Game hooks; the mode machine; no DOM at import\nsrc/core/                 actions.js look.js tuning.js save.js rng.js state.js events.js\nsrc/world/                terrain.js roads.js sites.js grass.js sky.js fire.js wind.js map-texture.js\nsrc/actors/               player.js soldier.js squad.js buddy.js civilian.js fauna.js rig.js\nsrc/combat/               weapons.js projectiles.js damage.js viewmodel.js\nsrc/body/                 health.js fever.js surgery.js\nsrc/vehicles/             vehicle.js driving.js repair.js train.js\nsrc/missions/             runner.js steps.js scripts.js ledger.js\nsrc/ui/                   hud.js menu.js screens/*.js glyphs.js prompts.js dialogue.js pixfont.js touchgate.js about.js\nsrc/dev/                  devmode.js sliders.js overlays.js\nsrc/attract/              director.js beats.js\nsrc/net/                  session.js hubSession.js linkSession.js soloSession.js round.js host.js client.js card.js\nsrc/audio/                sfx.js music.js voice.js ambience.js\nsrc/art/                  models.js textures.js viewmodels.js clips.js   (data only: exports for check.mjs --sheets)\nsrc/content/              title.js weapons.js sites.js roads.js missions.js dialogue.js notes.js about.js tuning-defaults.js\ntests/                    run.mjs lib/ suites/\n```\n\n**Patterns to follow:**\n- **One Game object, one Engine.** `createGame()` builds the world once. Title, attract, campaign and round are modes on it (`game.mode`). Don't use `engine.loadGame` between them: it would throw away the terrain. For New Game and Attract use `world.reset()`, which clears fire, sites and actors and repaints the base colours.\n- **Data over code.** All content lives in `src/content/` and `src/art/` as plain data. Adding a weapon, site, mission, line of dialogue or model must not need a code change. This is what lets the rest of the game be built later \"with normal designer tools\": tables, the sheets tool and dev mode's placement readout. Keep a one-page `DESIGNERS.md` in the dev folder saying how to add each.\n- **Actions, not keys.** `src/core/actions.js` maps an action to its pad code and its keyboard/mouse codes (section 16). All gameplay asks for actions. In `fixedUpdate` use `input.consumeAny(codes)`; in `update` use `wasPressed`.\n- **Gameplay in `fixedUpdate`, presentation in `update`.** All randomness comes from `ctx.random.fork(name)`, never `Math.random`. Cosmetic forks are made in `setup`.\n- **`TUNING`.** Every balance number lives in `src/content/tuning-defaults.js`, copied to a live `TUNING` object. Systems read `TUNING.x` at the moment of use and never cache it, so sliders act instantly.\n- **`game.state()`.** One plain JSON snapshot for every test: `{ mode, screen, focus, device, glyphSet, notice, attract, mission, step, hp, stones, tablets, syrettes, weapon:{id,cond,jammed,mag}, fever, pos, yaw, vehicle, hour, wind, fire:{burning,burnt}, prompts:[...], subtitle, buddies, ledger }`. Expose it in `ready(ctx)` as `window.__GAME__ = game` when `window` exists. Give `status()` a useful one-liner and `fingerprint()` a hash of hp, stones, weapon state, fire counts and actor positions.\n- **Sim and view apart.** A soldier is data plus an optional view; a fire cell is data plus sprites. In a round, a client's soldiers are puppets with no brain and no collider, driven by snapshots. Write them this way from the first day, because retrofitting it is the expensive part of section 19.\n- **Simulation bubble.** Sites within 260 m of the player are live; others are counters. Vehicles on patrol are simulated within 400 m. This keeps the code simple and headless tests fast.\n- **Guard the page.** Any code that touches `window`, `document`, `navigator` or `localStorage` checks that it exists. `src/game.js` must import cleanly in Node.\n- **Issues log.** Keep `tests/ISSUES.md`: one line per bug testing catches, marked critical or not. Your brief asks how many there were at the end.\n\n**Working rules from your brief:** keep every file inside your own folder (not `/tmp`, not a parent). Don't read other agents' folders. Other agents share this machine, so keep to one or two browsers at once and close them. Don't run `playwright install`; use `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder. `uv run --with pillow ...` is the way to get Python packages. Stop only processes you started, by PID, and never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`. \"Target closed\" or \"page crashed\" means memory ran out: run fewer browsers rather than retrying. If you serve anything, confirm the page's title is your game before trusting a result.\n\n---\n\n## 5. World\n\nThe whole game happens on one continuous 1280 × 1280 m terrain (x and z from -640 to 640, +x east, -z north, 512 × 512 cells at 2.5 m). Heights have a +40 offset.\n\n**Height function,** built up in this order:\n1. `40 + 14*fbm(x*.0032, z*.0032) + 2.5*fbm(x*.02, z*.02)`.\n2. The north ridge: up to +42 m with ridged noise around (-130, -520), radius 260.\n3. The west escarpment: rising +30 m for x < -520, cut by the Pass at (-590, -430).\n4. The salt pan: flattened to y = 36 around (200, 470), radius 150.\n5. The mine: a terraced pit at (150, -300), radius 70, 24 m deep.\n6. The river: a channel along its polyline, 14 m wide and 3 m below the banks.\n7. The stamps: flatten around each site (radial falloff) and along each road (5 m wide, 3 m shoulder).\n\nGenerate once in `setup`; don't run erosion.\n\n**Ground colour:** per cell after generating, in palette colours: sand and orange for grass, red for dirt and roads, plum for rock (slope), mist and white for the salt pan, green for riverbank and thorn woodland, teal near water. Keep a copy of the base colours for scars and regrowth.\n\n**Water:** one `WaterSurface` strip per river segment, with river depth 1 m everywhere. Wading halves your speed and stops sprinting. Vehicles cross only at the two bridges and the ford.\n\n**Sites.** All in `src/content/sites.js` as data. Tune positions by eye; the arc of the East Siding, the Cut, the Long Field and the Pass is the one thing that must stay (it's the plot).\n\n| Site | At (x, z) | What it is |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Mile Forty | (0, 40) | Truce town, no-fire zone radius 70 until mission M7: inside it your gun is slung and won't fire, and nobody shoots. Station, clinic, Tibor's shed, bar, market, notice board |\n| Pump Shed | (-120, 150) | First safehouse, empty |\n| Ranger Post | (40, -170) | Concord post with a cage, becomes a safehouse after M2 |\n| Water Tower | (470, -180) | Safehouse, guarded by 6 until cleared |\n| Fort Clement | (-430, -180) | Concord HQ: colonial stone walls (library `stone-wall`, `battlement`), parade ground, Vance's office |\n| The Long Field | (-260, -300) | Concord farmland, 180 × 120 m of tall dry crop, ammo bunker |\n| The Granary | (400, 280) | Harvest HQ: silos, tin sheds, Ansa's schoolroom |\n| East Siding | (430, -60) | Harvest fuel depot on the railway, 3 tanks |\n| Three Wells | (-260, 240) | Village, 12 civilians, wells, cistern |\n| Pump Station | (-330, 395) | On the river, feeds Three Wells |\n| Salt Pan | (200, 470) | Payroll road across white flats |\n| The Cut | (150, -300) | Open-pit mine: headframe, sluice house, spoil heaps |\n| Observatory | (-130, -520) | Harrow's trig station on the ridge |\n| The Pass | (-590, -430) | Mined border pass; the end |\n\n**Checkpoints** (posts of 4 to 6 soldiers, sandbags, a boom gate, sometimes a technical): P1 (90, -80) north road · P2 (-200, 30) west road · P3 (170, 90) east road · P4 (-320, -90) fort road · P5 (280, -210) mine road · P6 (300, 390) salt road. A cleared post re-mans after `TUNING.postRemanHours` (24 game hours); the source's instant respawn is one of its known annoyances, so we don't copy it.\n\n**Roads** (polylines in `src/content/roads.js`, painted red, flattened):\n- Mile Forty to P1, Ranger Post, P5, the Cut.\n- Mile Forty to P2, Three Wells, Pump Station.\n- P2 to P4, Fort Clement, the Long Field.\n- Mile Forty to P3 to the bridge at (250, 80), then forking to the Granary and to East Siding, then on to the Water Tower.\n- The Granary to P6 to the Salt Pan.\n- The back road: Mile Forty over the bridge at (120, 230) to P6.\n- The Long Field by a track to the Pass.\n- The Observatory by a switchback track from the Cut.\n\n**The railway:** a polyline from (-640, 110) through Mile Forty (0, 70) to East Siding (430, -60) and on to (640, -110), with a spur north to the Cut. Rails are `mine-rail` pieces or two long boxes, with their own bridge where they cross the river near (270, -5). Four **rail halts** (West Cutting, Mile Forty, East Siding, Cut Spur).\n\n**The Mercy (river):** (600,-640) → (420,-320) → (300,-120) → (250,80) → (120,230) → (-60,300) → (-240,380) → (-420,430) → (-640,470). Road bridges at (250, 80) and (120, 230); a ford at (-60, 300), which a track from Mile Forty uses.\n\n**Plants and scatter** (seeded, stored per cell for the fire):\n- About 400 trees (`acacia` shapes and a fat bottle tree), denser in the east woodland. Each has a `addStaticCylinder` trunk.\n- Thorn bushes (library `bush` recoloured).\n- Termite mounds (own model).\n- Boulders and `rock` on the ridge. Library `dead-tree`.\n- Reeds on the river.\n\n**Grass:** `GrassField` chunks of 48 × 48 m with about 7,000 blades, `mask` from the fuel map, `ground` from `heightAt`. Build a chunk the first time it comes within 170 m of the camera, hide it beyond 200 m, dispose beyond 320 m. On build, consult the fire grid (burnt cells get no blades).\n\n**Buildings** are `model()` defs with `collider:'parts'` so doorways stay open. Single storey with open doorways; no doors, no ladders. Watchtowers carry a soldier the player can shoot but not climb to.\n\n**Fauna:** gazelle herds in open grass (a small quadruped on its own rig with a keyed run cycle, fleeing gunshots and fire) and vultures circling burnt or corpse-heavy sites (`Boids` with a tiny flapping model). They are atmosphere and can't hurt you.\n\n**Civilians:** walk between points in Mile Forty and Three Wells, using the hero clips. Flee on gunfire. They cannot be killed by the player's bullets (hits are ignored and they flee), and fire makes them flee; the Ledger counts displacement, not bodies.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Fire, wind, time, weather\n\n**The fire grid** is the game's signature system, so write it first and test it hardest.\n\n- 256 × 256 cells at 5 m (`src/world/fire.js`). Arrays: `fuel` (0 none, 1 grass, 2 scrub, 3 tree, 4 building), `state` (0 fresh, 1 burning, 2 burnt, 3 regrowing), `burnEnd` and `burntAt`. Road, water, rock, salt and dirt have no fuel.\n- Tick at 4 Hz in `fixedUpdate` (every 15 steps), using `ctx.random.fork('fire')`. Keep a list of burning cells; never scan the grid.\n- Each burning cell tries its 8 neighbours with `p = 0.02 * fuelFactor * windFactor * slopeFactor * rainFactor * TUNING.fireSpread`, divided by 1.41 for diagonals. I calibrated 0.02 by simulating this grid: on flat grass the head, flank and back of the fire move at 1.9, 1.9, 1.9 m/s with no wind, 3.9, 2.4, 0.35 at 5 m/s, and 4.9, 2.9, 0.4 at 8 m/s. Larger bases run away quickly (0.08 gives about 10 m/s and burns upwind too).\n  - `windFactor = clamp(1 + TUNING.windEffect * windSpeed/5 * 1.6 * cos(angle between neighbour direction and wind), 0.15, 3.5)`\n  - `slopeFactor` = 1.4 uphill, 0.7 downhill. `rainFactor` = 0.15 while raining. `fuelFactor`: grass 1, scrub 0.8, tree 0.6, building 0.4.\n- Strong wind (above 6 m/s) gives an ember jump: small chance to light a fuel cell 2 to 3 cells downwind, which is how fire crosses a road.\n- Burn time: grass 8 to 14 s, scrub 12 to 18 s, tree and building 30 to 40 s.\n- **A test must measure the head of the fire at wind 5 m/s and require 2 to 5 m/s,** with flanks clearly slower, so a sprinting or running player can escape and a walking one (3.6 m/s) struggles.","numLines":200,"startLine":150,"totalLines":1129}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VmHC58hFuuHoLpHepxKcM2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgke1V8zSw1rKCARTAyi1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0126fXkoUWdL1vQrokXo34FJ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162033__x07393c/work/PLAN.md","offset":350,"limit":250},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9427,"cache_read_input_tokens":34607,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9427},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"feff64bd-df6d-45b2-b337-83604d3a2d73","uuid":"61b567b0-ce8a-4444-87ff-ed9a9241e001","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:20:46.255Z","request_id":"req_011Cgke1Uu7D3NuaxgL2sT1t","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0126fXkoUWdL1vQrokXo34FJ":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162033__x07393c/work/PLAN.md","offset":350,"limit":250}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0126fXkoUWdL1vQrokXo34FJ","type":"tool_result","content":"350\t- **Burnt land can't burn again.** That is mechanics and plot at once.\n351\t- `ignite(x, z, radius)` is the only way in. Used by flare, molotov, flamethrower, explosions, burning soldiers, wrecks and tracer fire on full-auto from the mounted MG (small chance).\n352\t- `burnStrip(polylineId, width)` quietly burns a named strip, for story scars (section 14).\n353\t\n354\t**Effects on the world:**\n355\t- A cell going burnt recolours its terrain cells to a speckled ink, night and slate mix, zeroes its grass blades, and swaps any tree to a scorched version (bare black trunk, built by `palette` swap, plus `dead-tree`).\n356\t- After `TUNING.regrowHours` (48 game hours) a burnt cell goes to state 3: painted green and lime with short new grass. *The green comes back faster than the people.* It stays state 3 (can't burn) until the next save or day-roll, then back to fresh fuel at half density.\n357\t- Actors in a burning cell catch fire: soldiers panic (section 10), players burn (section 9).\n358\t- Vehicles in fire lose 6 HP/s. Fuel tanks and drums explode when their cell burns, igniting a radius and throwing a `physics.explode` and `screen.shockwave`.\n359\t\n360\t**What you see:**\n361\t- A `SpriteBatch` of flames: 3 per burning cell within 220 m of the camera, at seeded offsets, frame-flipped every 0.1 s.\n362\t- `particles.register` smoke columns per fire cluster (cluster = 16 × 16 cell tile), embers, ash drifting downwind.\n363\t- Up to 3 `ctx.lights.request` over the nearest clusters (intensity by size, `flicker:'torch'`), released when the cluster dies.\n364\t- Sound: one spatialized `audio.loop` for the nearest cluster, volume by size (section 22).\n365\t- Burn scars show on your held map.\n366\t\n367\t**Save:** run-length encode `state` into the save JSON.\n368\t\n369\t**Wind.** One vector, a slow random walk (direction changes by up to 15° a minute, speed 0 to 8 m/s). It drives `grass.wind`, `WindUniforms` (trees via `swayObject`) and rain. Read it from the grass, from a strip of red cloth on every post (`clothGrid` + `SoftMesh`), and from a small wind arrow on the GPS. Missions can pin it (`wind` field).\n370\t\n371\t**Time.** Full day = `TUNING.dayMinutes` (30 real minutes). Call `applySky(engine, skyAt(hour))` at up to 10 Hz. Paint the dome from this table, interpolated:\n372\t\n373\t| Hour | Top | Middle | Horizon |\n374\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n375\t| 5.5 | `#3a4a6b` | `#d99a6c` | `#f0b070` |\n376\t| 9 | `#6f9fb5` | `#c9d6c4` | `#e3cf9e` |\n377\t| 13 | `#7fa6b8` | `#d8dcc0` | `#ecd9a6` |\n378\t| 16.5 | `#7fa6b8` | `#e9c98f` | `#d98a4e` |\n379\t| 19 | `#2b2f52` | `#8a4f5c` | `#c8643c` |\n380\t| 22 | `#0b0f20` | `#141a2e` | `#1c2238` |\n381\t\n382\tAdd a sun disc (`glowMaterial`), a moon, and stars at night (a `SpriteBatch` following the camera). Night cuts AI vision (section 10), which is why sleeping until midnight is a tactic.\n383\t\n384\t**Weather.** Dry by default. A dust wind (`particles`, stronger wind, haze raised) and a storm (`Precipitation` rain, lightning `screen.flash` + thunder, fire damped). Storms are rare, scripted for M9, and available from dev mode.\n385\t\n386\t**The look.** The engine's native pixel pipeline plus a light grade. Keep these filters always on and change only parameters, since parameters are free and adding a filter rebuilds the pipeline:\n387\t```js\n388\texport const LOOK = {\n389\t  scene: [{ id:'adjust', params:{ brightness:0, contrast:1.06, saturation:1.05, warmth:0.04 } }, { id:'chromatic', params:{ spread:0 } }, { id:'vignette', params:{ intensity:0.28, softness:0.7, amount:1 } }],\n390\t  actors: { pixel:{ size:1, outline:0.45, crease:0.08 }, filters:[] },\n391\t  environment: { pixel:{ size:1, outline:0.32, crease:0.05 }, filters:[] },\n392\t};\n393\t```\n394\tFever, low health and flames adjust `spread`, `saturation`, `warmth` and `intensity` through `engine.setLook` (at up to 15 Hz).\n395\t\n396\t---\n397\t\n398\t## 7. Player\n399\t\n400\tA `CharacterController` capsule with `eyePosition` at feet + 1.55 (crouched 0.95). No visible body. Head-bob and landing dip go in the view group, not the camera.\n401\t\n402\t| Move | Speed (m/s) | Notes |\n403\t| --- | --- | --- |\n404\t| Walk (stick partly tilted; Caps Lock toggles it on keyboard, never Alt, since Alt+D jumps to the address bar) | 3.6 | Quiet |\n405\t| Run (default) | 5.4 | Heard at 25 m |\n406\t| Sprint (click L3 or hold Shift while moving forward) | 7.4 | 6 s of stamina, 8 s to refill, heard at 40 m, can't fire or aim |\n407\t| Crouch | 2.0 | Quiet; hidden in tall grass |\n408\t| Wading | 2.7 | No sprint |\n409\t\n410\tJump: `jumpSpeed` 8 (about 1.3 m with gravity -24), enough for low walls. Fall damage above 6 m. No prone, no ladders, no ledge grabs.\n411\t\n412\t**Use.** Scan for usable things within 2.5 m and 25° of the view centre, and show one prompt: glyph plus verb (`[glyph] OPEN THE BONNET`). Some are hold-to-use with a ring that fills.\n413\t\n414\t**In a menu or with the map cursor active,** the look stick does nothing. Otherwise the world never pauses except in the pause menu.\n415\t\n416\t**Aim assist** (pad only, on by default, Settings toggle): within 4° of a live enemy's torso, look sensitivity falls 35%; ADS adds a slight pull. Pad look gets a response curve (`tilt^1.6`) on top of the engine's delta.\n417\t\n418\t---\n419\t\n420\t## 8. Weapons and wear\n421\t\n422\t**Slots:** melee (always), sidearm, main, special, plus throwables, plus a mounted MG when on a technical. You carry one of each slot, swapped at safehouse lockers or by picking guns off the ground.\n423\t\n424\t| id | Name | Slot | Mag / reserve | Rate | Damage | Range | Wear per shot | Stones | Notes |\n425\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n426\t| `cane` | Cane knife | melee | - | 1.6/s | 55; kills from behind | 2.2 m | - | start | Quiet |\n427\t| `m38` | Service revolver | sidearm | 6 / 36 | 3/s | 34 | 45 m | 0.5 | start | Dependable |\n428\t| `flare` | Flare pistol | sidearm | 1 / 8 | 0.6/s | 12 + ignite | arcs to 70 m | 0 | given in M3 | Gravity projectile; lights fuel where it lands; sets soldiers alight |\n429\t| `tractor` | \"Tractor\" rifle | main | 30 / 150 | 9/s | 24 | 110 m | 0.35 | 6 | Every soldier has one, mostly rusted |\n430\t| `mule` | \"Mule\" pump gun | main | 6 / 36 | 1.1/s | 10 pellets × 9 | 22 m | 0.8 | 8 | |\n431\t| `empire` | \"Old Empire\" bolt rifle | main | 5 / 30 | 0.8/s | 95 | 320 m | 1.0 | 14 | 6× scope, FOV 12 |\n432\t| `weed` | \"Weed Burner\" flamethrower | special | 120 / 240 fuel | stream | 28/s + ignite | 14 m | 0.12 per unit | 18 | Lights everything in its cone; below condition 20 it can leak and light you |\n433\t| `tube` | \"Long Tube\" rocket launcher | special | 1 / 5 | 0.4/s | 180, blast 6 m | 160 m | 2 | 20 | Lights the blast; wrecks vehicles |\n434\t| `mg` | Mounted MG | technical | no ammo; overheats at 6 s, cools in 4 s | 10/s | 20 | 180 m | - | - | |\n435\t| `molotov` | Bottle | throw | carry 3 | - | ignite radius 4 m | throw to 25 m | - | 1 each | |\n436\t| `charge` | Remote charge | throw | carry 2 | - | 220, blast 7 m | placed | - | 3 each | Plant by holding; second press detonates |\n437\t\n438\tHip fire has a small spread; L2/RMB aims (FOV 62 to 45; `empire` to 12) with tighter spread and recoil. Hitscan for bullets using `physics.castRay`, with the collider-to-actor map from section 10; rockets, flares and bottles are projectiles integrated per fixed step with a ray per step. Head ×2.5, legs ×0.7, by hit height on the capsule.\n439\t\n440\t**Wear** is the source's spirit made precise:\n441\t- Every gun has `cond` 0 to 100. Guns from a safehouse locker start at 100; guns off the ground start at 20 to 55.\n442\t- Each shot: `cond -= wearPerShot * TUNING.wearRate * (upgrade ? 0.6 : 1)`.\n443\t- Per shot, jam chance: `TUNING.jamRate * 0.12 * (1 - cond/100)^2`. That gives about 1 in 800 at 90, 1 in 33 at 50, 1 in 17 at 30.\n444\t- **A jam:** trigger clicks, the gun stays jammed until you press Reload 2 to 4 times (seeded). Each press plays a slap-and-rack animation. Soldiers' guns jam too, and they swear about it (bark plus a pause, an opening for you).\n445\t- **Thresholds shown on the gun itself, with no number on the HUD:** at 60 and above it's clean; 30 to 59 rust parts appear (extra named parts toggled visible, red/plum on the barrel and receiver); below 30 tape and a smoke puff on firing; below 10 a rattle; at 0 it **falls apart** (a short animation, the magazine and a piece drop, the slot is empty).\n446\t- A small gun icon by the ammo count goes from white to orange to red, the only HUD hint.\n447\t\n448\t**Feedback:** `engine.shake.add`, `hitstop(0.02)` on a hit, muzzle `lights.request({ lifetime:0.06 })`, tracer (`Trail` or sprite) on every third round, `particles.burst('impact')` plus red pixel blood, `Decals` for scorches, hit marker (small X) and rumble. Body-shot soldiers play `HitReact`; a killing shot switches to `Ragdoll` with the bullet's push.\n449\t\n450\t---\n451\t\n452\t## 9. The body\n453\t\n454\t**Health** is 100, shown as 5 segments. It regenerates only to the top of the current segment, after 5 s without damage.\n455\t- **Syrette:** L1/Q, hold 0.8 s, +40 HP. Carry 3 (upgrade to 5). The animation: thigh jab, thumb.\n456\t- **Field surgery:** below 20 HP the screen drains colour and a heartbeat starts. Pressing Heal runs one of four scenes (about 4 s, can't move or shoot, can be interrupted by another hit), and leaves you at 40 HP with no item used.\n457\t  1. Pliers pull a bullet from your forearm.\n458\t  2. A knife digs shrapnel from your thigh.\n459\t  3. You snap a dislocated finger back.\n460\t  4. A lighter cauterises a burn (used whenever the last damage was fire).\n461\t  Keyed with the hands rig (section 21), with pixel blood, sound and a grunt. These are a highlight, so make them good.\n462\t- **Burning:** catching fire does 8 HP/s plus orange flames at the screen edge until you leave fuel for 2 s or reach water.\n463\t- **Down at 0:** if a buddy is available, a rescue scene plays (section 12). Otherwise: *YOU DIED IN THE SORREL. THE PAPERWORK WILL TAKE LONGER.* Then Continue (last save) or Title.\n464\t\n465\t**Blackwater fever.** The source's malaria idea, with a clock you can feel.\n466\t- `TUNING.feverMinutes` (default 12) ± 2 between attacks; the interval shrinks by 1 minute after each act. First attack scripted in M0.\n467\t- **Onset:** view sways, `chromatic.spread` climbs to 4, saturation falls, warmth up, heartbeat, a line in the subtitle band (*THE FEVER.* or variants), and the prompt `[glyph] TABLET`.\n468\t- **With a tablet** (D-pad Down/H, hold 1 s, hand-to-mouth animation): clears over 5 s.\n469\t- **Ignored for 25 s, or no tablets:** vision narrows, you fall, and you wake at the nearest safehouse or the clinic hours later, with 20% of your stones gone. Buddy if available: they carry you and complain.\n470\t- **Supply:** 3 after M1; Mercy Runs pay 4 each (section 13). Tablets can't be bought with stones. That is the point: Odile wants your time.\n471\t\n472\t---\n473\t\n474\t## 10. Soldiers and AI\n475\t\n476\t**Bodies:** the soldier model in faction palettes (several `skin` values and hat variants), a rifle parented to `HandR`, a `CharacterController` capsule (speed from role), `physics.tag(collider, 'npc')` and a `Map` from collider handle to actor. Clips are in section 21.\n477\t\n478\t**Roles:** rifle (most), mg (slower, heavier burst), sniper (towers, long range), rocket (rare), driver.\n479\t\n480\t**Perception.**\n481\t- Vision cone 120°, range `TUNING.enemyVision` (90 m) × stance (crouch 0.55, in tall grass 0.6) × light (night 0.45, doubled if you are lit by fire or muzzle flash).\n482\t- LOS by `physics.castRay` to head and chest.\n483\t- A suspicion meter fills over about 1.5 s by distance. States: `idle → suspicious → alert → searching → idle`.\n484\t- Hearing: gunshot radius per weapon (rifle 120 m), sprint 40 m, run 25 m.\n485\t- Squad members share the last known position.\n486\t- Close by, a small pixel `?` or `!` floats over the head (HUD text projected through the camera). That is the only detection UI.\n487\t\n488\t**Hostility.** Everyone outside Mile Forty shoots you on sight, with one exception: **while you hold a contract from a faction, you carry its pass**, and its soldiers leave you alone unless you fire near them or walk into their HQ's inner yard. Choosing whose ground to cross becomes a real decision.\n489\t\n490\t**Brain:** a `BehaviorTree` for each soldier, ticked at 10 Hz, in priority order:\n491\t1. **On fire:** run in panic (`BurnPanic` clip), scream in gibberish, spread fire to the cells they cross, die in about 5 s.\n492\t2. **Fire near and upwind:** flee crosswind.\n493\t3. **Grenade or rocket nearby:** dodge.\n494\t4. **In combat:** take cover (site cover points, then nearest tree or prop within 12 m), fire bursts of 3 to 5, re-position every 4 to 8 s; one squad member flanks by `NavGrid.path`.\n495\t5. **Searching:** walk to the last known position, look about, give up after 20 s.\n496\t6. **Duty:** patrol, stand guard, sit by a fire, sleep at night.\n497\t\n498\tPathing uses a global `NavGrid` at 2 m (slope, water, buildings closed), budgeted per section 3; off the grid or far away they walk straight.\n499\t\n500\t**Shooting at you:** each round has an aim error `lerp(1.2°, 5°, dist/80) / TUNING.enemyAccuracy`, ×1.4 if you are moving fast. Damage per bullet 9. Reaction 0.45 s.\n501\t\n502\t**Vehicles:** patrol technicals follow road polylines with a PD steering controller, chase if alert, and their crews dismount at 25 m.\n503\t\n504\t**Barks** (gibberish voice plus a short subtitle, spatial): at least 15 per faction: spotted, lost you, grenade, jammed, fire, ally down. Examples in section 15.\n505\t\n506\t---\n507\t\n508\t## 11. Vehicles\n509\t\n510\tThree vehicles on `Vehicle` (tuning in section 3; truck heavier and slower): **pickup**, **technical** (pickup with MG on a pintle), **cargo truck** (also a tanker variant for M3). All have `model()` bodies, four wheel meshes posed by `wheelPose`, and suspension from the engine. One train: a kinematic body moved along the railway polyline, with flatcars.\n511\t\n512\t**Driving in first person:** eye at the driver's seat, your hands on a wheel that turns with steering, a dash, a small arrow on the dash pointing to your waypoint. R3/C toggles a chase camera (computed in your `step`, via `cam.override`). You can raise the map while driving, as you should: it's the most famous moment in this kind of game, and it's dangerous.\n513\t\n514\t**Getting in and out:** Use at a door. The player's capsule is disabled while seated and placed by the door on exit. Triangle/F swaps to the technical's gun; the truck rolls on with no driver, and that is your problem.\n515\t\n516\t**Damage:** 100 HP, from bullets, rockets, fire and collisions (over 8 m/s). Below 35: smoke, power × 0.6. Below 10: flames, explodes in 8 s unless you bail. At 0 and not on fire: stalled. **Repair:** stand at the bonnet, hold Use 3 s (wrench animation with sparks), back to 50 HP. The fixed truck coughs for a few seconds. Vehicles in M1 are scripted to break so everyone learns it.\n517\t\n518\tParking at a safehouse repairs fully overnight.\n519\t\n520\t---\n521\t\n522\t## 12. Buddies\n523\t\n524\tThree hired friends, each a named person with a real arc. All live in the bar at Mile Forty or at safehouses once you've met them.\n525\t\n526\t| | Ines Carvalho | Dov Halpern | Mags Oyelaran |\n527\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n528\t| Met | Freed from the cage, M2 | Tibor's shed, after M2; his plan in M4 | Clinic, after your first Mercy Run |\n529\t| Look | Flight cap, sunglasses, sand jacket | Bald, bandoliers, bad left hand | Braids, olive vest, medic's satchel with green cross |\n530\t| Perk | Rescues you by truck, from farther away | Gives 2 free charges per mission | +1 syrette at any safehouse; rescues you with 60 HP instead of 40 |\n531\t| Offers the alternate plan in | M3 | M4 | M5 |\n532\t| Voice | Mid, quick | Low, slow | High, warm, never stops |\n533\t\n534\t**Alternate plans.** On certain contracts the buddy calls (radio squelch, subtitles) with a better way, adding objectives and more risk, in exchange for bonus stones and +1 trust. You can accept or ignore them on the spot. If you take it, they fight beside you on that mission.\n535\t\n536\t**Rescue.** At HP 0 with a buddy available: the screen drops, and you are dragged backward in first person (low camera, sky, their boots, their gunfire) while they swear at you; then you are up at 40 HP (Mags 60) with them next to you, and the buddy is spent for 1 game day. 15% chance they take a wound and are out for 2 days. They remind you of it later.\n537\t\n538\t**Down.** A buddy fighting with you has 200 HP. At 0 they fall, wounded, for 40 s, marked by a floating pixel cross. At their side, a two-button prompt:\n539\t- `[Square/E] SYRETTE` (uses one; they're up at 50%, trust +1)\n540\t- `[Triangle/F] FINISH IT` (one shot, your sidearm's animation)\n541\t\n542\tIf you have no syrettes, only the second option is lit. If the 40 s run out, they die anyway. If they are burning, they ask you to finish it. M6 forces this moment at least once.\n543\t\n544\tA dead buddy stays dead: name scratched on the safehouse wall, their gun leaning under it, one Ledger line, and no more of their lines anywhere. The game must play on without any of them. Write their missions so the alternate plan just doesn't arrive.\n545\t\n546\t**At the end,** every surviving buddy comes to the Pass for the stones (section 14). Those with trust 3 or more first offer to split it, then draw anyway if you refuse.\n547\t\n548\t---\n549\t\n550\t## 13. Stones, safehouses, side content\n551\t\n552\t**Rough stones** are the money.\n553\t- Missions pay 5 to 25; cases hold 2 to 6.\n554\t- Total available in the demo is about 180; everything for sale costs about 130.\n555\t- Spent at **Tibor's** (a gun rack and a table).\n556\t\n557\t**Tibor's stock:** the guns in section 8 (buying one unlocks clean copies at every locker for free, forever), bottles, charges, and four upgrades:\n558\t- Cleaning kit (6 stones): wear × 0.6.\n559\t- Syrette pouch (5): carry 5.\n560\t- Ammo belt (5): +50% reserve.\n561\t- Vehicle kit (6): repair in 1.5 s.\n562\t\n563\t**The tracker.** Given in M4. D-pad Left/T raises it in your left hand: a small device with an LED and a beep that speeds up and turns green within 60 m of a stone case. Twelve cases are hidden around the Kettle (wrecks, hollow trees, under a dead man's truck, in guarded sheds).\n564\t\n565\t**Safehouses** (3):\n566\t- **Bed:** sleep to Dawn, Noon, Dusk or Midnight, and it saves.\n567\t- **Locker:** set your loadout.\n568\t- **Crate:** ammo and syrettes.\n569\t- A wall for names. Clear the guards first where there are any. Buddies wait here with offers.\n570\t\n571\t**Rail halts** (4): fast travel once visited, 1 stone, 1 game hour. It costs you Old Bakr's latest thought on a black screen with clacking wheels.\n572\t\n573\t**Trig points** (6): stone pillars on hilltops. Use one: a new note in Harrow's hand appears in your map's margin, and you can read it on the map. All six: he leaves you a clean `empire` at a locker. A note that says so.\n574\t\n575\t**Mercy Runs** (3, from Odile, plus M1): carry a crate to a marked place within 5 minutes. Pays 4 tablets. Fail and she says nothing, which is worse.\n576\t\n577\t**The Ledger.** Create/J in play, also in the pause menu, and at the end. One sheet, five big lines, at most (never a table):\n578\t- Hectares burned (burnt cells × 0.0025)\n579\t- People moved off their land\n580\t- Men you were paid to kill\n581\t- Friends lost\n582\t- Stones earned\n583\t\n584\tWritten as Harrow would write it.\n585\t\n586\t**Saving:** single slot, versioned JSON in `localStorage` (`src/core/save.js`), written on sleep and on mission complete, read through `ctx.input.send` so replays stay exact. Main menu's Continue loads it. Dev mode has Quick Save/Load.\n587\t\n588\t---\n589\t\n590\t## 14. Missions\n591\t\n592\t**Format** (`src/content/missions.js`), a sketch:\n593\t```js\n594\t{ id:'m3', title:'FUEL FOR THE PEACE', giver:'vance', requires:['m2'], pay:15, wind:{ dir:[-0.8,-0.6], speed:5 },\n595\t  brief:['vance:m3.brief.1','vance:m3.brief.2'],\n596\t  alt:{ buddy:'ines', call:'ines:m3.alt', bonus:5, steps:[ {do:'steal',tag:'tanker',text:'TAKE THE TANKER AT P3'} ] },\n597\t  steps:[ {do:'goto',site:'east-siding',r:140,text:'REACH THE SIDING'},\n598\t          {do:'destroy',tag:'fuel-tank',n:3,text:'BURN THE TANKS'},\n599\t          {do:'leave',site:'east-siding',r:220,text:'GET OUT'},"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"feff64bd-df6d-45b2-b337-83604d3a2d73","uuid":"8adcf97c-1756-4571-81fd-5f79b76c2de5","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:20:46.261Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162033__x07393c/work/PLAN.md","content":"- **Burnt land can't burn again.** That is mechanics and plot at once.\n- `ignite(x, z, radius)` is the only way in. Used by flare, molotov, flamethrower, explosions, burning soldiers, wrecks and tracer fire on full-auto from the mounted MG (small chance).\n- `burnStrip(polylineId, width)` quietly burns a named strip, for story scars (section 14).\n\n**Effects on the world:**\n- A cell going burnt recolours its terrain cells to a speckled ink, night and slate mix, zeroes its grass blades, and swaps any tree to a scorched version (bare black trunk, built by `palette` swap, plus `dead-tree`).\n- After `TUNING.regrowHours` (48 game hours) a burnt cell goes to state 3: painted green and lime with short new grass. *The green comes back faster than the people.* It stays state 3 (can't burn) until the next save or day-roll, then back to fresh fuel at half density.\n- Actors in a burning cell catch fire: soldiers panic (section 10), players burn (section 9).\n- Vehicles in fire lose 6 HP/s. Fuel tanks and drums explode when their cell burns, igniting a radius and throwing a `physics.explode` and `screen.shockwave`.\n\n**What you see:**\n- A `SpriteBatch` of flames: 3 per burning cell within 220 m of the camera, at seeded offsets, frame-flipped every 0.1 s.\n- `particles.register` smoke columns per fire cluster (cluster = 16 × 16 cell tile), embers, ash drifting downwind.\n- Up to 3 `ctx.lights.request` over the nearest clusters (intensity by size, `flicker:'torch'`), released when the cluster dies.\n- Sound: one spatialized `audio.loop` for the nearest cluster, volume by size (section 22).\n- Burn scars show on your held map.\n\n**Save:** run-length encode `state` into the save JSON.\n\n**Wind.** One vector, a slow random walk (direction changes by up to 15° a minute, speed 0 to 8 m/s). It drives `grass.wind`, `WindUniforms` (trees via `swayObject`) and rain. Read it from the grass, from a strip of red cloth on every post (`clothGrid` + `SoftMesh`), and from a small wind arrow on the GPS. Missions can pin it (`wind` field).\n\n**Time.** Full day = `TUNING.dayMinutes` (30 real minutes). Call `applySky(engine, skyAt(hour))` at up to 10 Hz. Paint the dome from this table, interpolated:\n\n| Hour | Top | Middle | Horizon |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 5.5 | `#3a4a6b` | `#d99a6c` | `#f0b070` |\n| 9 | `#6f9fb5` | `#c9d6c4` | `#e3cf9e` |\n| 13 | `#7fa6b8` | `#d8dcc0` | `#ecd9a6` |\n| 16.5 | `#7fa6b8` | `#e9c98f` | `#d98a4e` |\n| 19 | `#2b2f52` | `#8a4f5c` | `#c8643c` |\n| 22 | `#0b0f20` | `#141a2e` | `#1c2238` |\n\nAdd a sun disc (`glowMaterial`), a moon, and stars at night (a `SpriteBatch` following the camera). Night cuts AI vision (section 10), which is why sleeping until midnight is a tactic.\n\n**Weather.** Dry by default. A dust wind (`particles`, stronger wind, haze raised) and a storm (`Precipitation` rain, lightning `screen.flash` + thunder, fire damped). Storms are rare, scripted for M9, and available from dev mode.\n\n**The look.** The engine's native pixel pipeline plus a light grade. Keep these filters always on and change only parameters, since parameters are free and adding a filter rebuilds the pipeline:\n```js\nexport const LOOK = {\n  scene: [{ id:'adjust', params:{ brightness:0, contrast:1.06, saturation:1.05, warmth:0.04 } }, { id:'chromatic', params:{ spread:0 } }, { id:'vignette', params:{ intensity:0.28, softness:0.7, amount:1 } }],\n  actors: { pixel:{ size:1, outline:0.45, crease:0.08 }, filters:[] },\n  environment: { pixel:{ size:1, outline:0.32, crease:0.05 }, filters:[] },\n};\n```\nFever, low health and flames adjust `spread`, `saturation`, `warmth` and `intensity` through `engine.setLook` (at up to 15 Hz).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Player\n\nA `CharacterController` capsule with `eyePosition` at feet + 1.55 (crouched 0.95). No visible body. Head-bob and landing dip go in the view group, not the camera.\n\n| Move | Speed (m/s) | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Walk (stick partly tilted; Caps Lock toggles it on keyboard, never Alt, since Alt+D jumps to the address bar) | 3.6 | Quiet |\n| Run (default) | 5.4 | Heard at 25 m |\n| Sprint (click L3 or hold Shift while moving forward) | 7.4 | 6 s of stamina, 8 s to refill, heard at 40 m, can't fire or aim |\n| Crouch | 2.0 | Quiet; hidden in tall grass |\n| Wading | 2.7 | No sprint |\n\nJump: `jumpSpeed` 8 (about 1.3 m with gravity -24), enough for low walls. Fall damage above 6 m. No prone, no ladders, no ledge grabs.\n\n**Use.** Scan for usable things within 2.5 m and 25° of the view centre, and show one prompt: glyph plus verb (`[glyph] OPEN THE BONNET`). Some are hold-to-use with a ring that fills.\n\n**In a menu or with the map cursor active,** the look stick does nothing. Otherwise the world never pauses except in the pause menu.\n\n**Aim assist** (pad only, on by default, Settings toggle): within 4° of a live enemy's torso, look sensitivity falls 35%; ADS adds a slight pull. Pad look gets a response curve (`tilt^1.6`) on top of the engine's delta.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Weapons and wear\n\n**Slots:** melee (always), sidearm, main, special, plus throwables, plus a mounted MG when on a technical. You carry one of each slot, swapped at safehouse lockers or by picking guns off the ground.\n\n| id | Name | Slot | Mag / reserve | Rate | Damage | Range | Wear per shot | Stones | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `cane` | Cane knife | melee | - | 1.6/s | 55; kills from behind | 2.2 m | - | start | Quiet |\n| `m38` | Service revolver | sidearm | 6 / 36 | 3/s | 34 | 45 m | 0.5 | start | Dependable |\n| `flare` | Flare pistol | sidearm | 1 / 8 | 0.6/s | 12 + ignite | arcs to 70 m | 0 | given in M3 | Gravity projectile; lights fuel where it lands; sets soldiers alight |\n| `tractor` | \"Tractor\" rifle | main | 30 / 150 | 9/s | 24 | 110 m | 0.35 | 6 | Every soldier has one, mostly rusted |\n| `mule` | \"Mule\" pump gun | main | 6 / 36 | 1.1/s | 10 pellets × 9 | 22 m | 0.8 | 8 | |\n| `empire` | \"Old Empire\" bolt rifle | main | 5 / 30 | 0.8/s | 95 | 320 m | 1.0 | 14 | 6× scope, FOV 12 |\n| `weed` | \"Weed Burner\" flamethrower | special | 120 / 240 fuel | stream | 28/s + ignite | 14 m | 0.12 per unit | 18 | Lights everything in its cone; below condition 20 it can leak and light you |\n| `tube` | \"Long Tube\" rocket launcher | special | 1 / 5 | 0.4/s | 180, blast 6 m | 160 m | 2 | 20 | Lights the blast; wrecks vehicles |\n| `mg` | Mounted MG | technical | no ammo; overheats at 6 s, cools in 4 s | 10/s | 20 | 180 m | - | - | |\n| `molotov` | Bottle | throw | carry 3 | - | ignite radius 4 m | throw to 25 m | - | 1 each | |\n| `charge` | Remote charge | throw | carry 2 | - | 220, blast 7 m | placed | - | 3 each | Plant by holding; second press detonates |\n\nHip fire has a small spread; L2/RMB aims (FOV 62 to 45; `empire` to 12) with tighter spread and recoil. Hitscan for bullets using `physics.castRay`, with the collider-to-actor map from section 10; rockets, flares and bottles are projectiles integrated per fixed step with a ray per step. Head ×2.5, legs ×0.7, by hit height on the capsule.\n\n**Wear** is the source's spirit made precise:\n- Every gun has `cond` 0 to 100. Guns from a safehouse locker start at 100; guns off the ground start at 20 to 55.\n- Each shot: `cond -= wearPerShot * TUNING.wearRate * (upgrade ? 0.6 : 1)`.\n- Per shot, jam chance: `TUNING.jamRate * 0.12 * (1 - cond/100)^2`. That gives about 1 in 800 at 90, 1 in 33 at 50, 1 in 17 at 30.\n- **A jam:** trigger clicks, the gun stays jammed until you press Reload 2 to 4 times (seeded). Each press plays a slap-and-rack animation. Soldiers' guns jam too, and they swear about it (bark plus a pause, an opening for you).\n- **Thresholds shown on the gun itself, with no number on the HUD:** at 60 and above it's clean; 30 to 59 rust parts appear (extra named parts toggled visible, red/plum on the barrel and receiver); below 30 tape and a smoke puff on firing; below 10 a rattle; at 0 it **falls apart** (a short animation, the magazine and a piece drop, the slot is empty).\n- A small gun icon by the ammo count goes from white to orange to red, the only HUD hint.\n\n**Feedback:** `engine.shake.add`, `hitstop(0.02)` on a hit, muzzle `lights.request({ lifetime:0.06 })`, tracer (`Trail` or sprite) on every third round, `particles.burst('impact')` plus red pixel blood, `Decals` for scorches, hit marker (small X) and rumble. Body-shot soldiers play `HitReact`; a killing shot switches to `Ragdoll` with the bullet's push.\n\n---\n\n## 9. The body\n\n**Health** is 100, shown as 5 segments. It regenerates only to the top of the current segment, after 5 s without damage.\n- **Syrette:** L1/Q, hold 0.8 s, +40 HP. Carry 3 (upgrade to 5). The animation: thigh jab, thumb.\n- **Field surgery:** below 20 HP the screen drains colour and a heartbeat starts. Pressing Heal runs one of four scenes (about 4 s, can't move or shoot, can be interrupted by another hit), and leaves you at 40 HP with no item used.\n  1. Pliers pull a bullet from your forearm.\n  2. A knife digs shrapnel from your thigh.\n  3. You snap a dislocated finger back.\n  4. A lighter cauterises a burn (used whenever the last damage was fire).\n  Keyed with the hands rig (section 21), with pixel blood, sound and a grunt. These are a highlight, so make them good.\n- **Burning:** catching fire does 8 HP/s plus orange flames at the screen edge until you leave fuel for 2 s or reach water.\n- **Down at 0:** if a buddy is available, a rescue scene plays (section 12). Otherwise: *YOU DIED IN THE SORREL. THE PAPERWORK WILL TAKE LONGER.* Then Continue (last save) or Title.\n\n**Blackwater fever.** The source's malaria idea, with a clock you can feel.\n- `TUNING.feverMinutes` (default 12) ± 2 between attacks; the interval shrinks by 1 minute after each act. First attack scripted in M0.\n- **Onset:** view sways, `chromatic.spread` climbs to 4, saturation falls, warmth up, heartbeat, a line in the subtitle band (*THE FEVER.* or variants), and the prompt `[glyph] TABLET`.\n- **With a tablet** (D-pad Down/H, hold 1 s, hand-to-mouth animation): clears over 5 s.\n- **Ignored for 25 s, or no tablets:** vision narrows, you fall, and you wake at the nearest safehouse or the clinic hours later, with 20% of your stones gone. Buddy if available: they carry you and complain.\n- **Supply:** 3 after M1; Mercy Runs pay 4 each (section 13). Tablets can't be bought with stones. That is the point: Odile wants your time.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Soldiers and AI\n\n**Bodies:** the soldier model in faction palettes (several `skin` values and hat variants), a rifle parented to `HandR`, a `CharacterController` capsule (speed from role), `physics.tag(collider, 'npc')` and a `Map` from collider handle to actor. Clips are in section 21.\n\n**Roles:** rifle (most), mg (slower, heavier burst), sniper (towers, long range), rocket (rare), driver.\n\n**Perception.**\n- Vision cone 120°, range `TUNING.enemyVision` (90 m) × stance (crouch 0.55, in tall grass 0.6) × light (night 0.45, doubled if you are lit by fire or muzzle flash).\n- LOS by `physics.castRay` to head and chest.\n- A suspicion meter fills over about 1.5 s by distance. States: `idle → suspicious → alert → searching → idle`.\n- Hearing: gunshot radius per weapon (rifle 120 m), sprint 40 m, run 25 m.\n- Squad members share the last known position.\n- Close by, a small pixel `?` or `!` floats over the head (HUD text projected through the camera). That is the only detection UI.\n\n**Hostility.** Everyone outside Mile Forty shoots you on sight, with one exception: **while you hold a contract from a faction, you carry its pass**, and its soldiers leave you alone unless you fire near them or walk into their HQ's inner yard. Choosing whose ground to cross becomes a real decision.\n\n**Brain:** a `BehaviorTree` for each soldier, ticked at 10 Hz, in priority order:\n1. **On fire:** run in panic (`BurnPanic` clip), scream in gibberish, spread fire to the cells they cross, die in about 5 s.\n2. **Fire near and upwind:** flee crosswind.\n3. **Grenade or rocket nearby:** dodge.\n4. **In combat:** take cover (site cover points, then nearest tree or prop within 12 m), fire bursts of 3 to 5, re-position every 4 to 8 s; one squad member flanks by `NavGrid.path`.\n5. **Searching:** walk to the last known position, look about, give up after 20 s.\n6. **Duty:** patrol, stand guard, sit by a fire, sleep at night.\n\nPathing uses a global `NavGrid` at 2 m (slope, water, buildings closed), budgeted per section 3; off the grid or far away they walk straight.\n\n**Shooting at you:** each round has an aim error `lerp(1.2°, 5°, dist/80) / TUNING.enemyAccuracy`, ×1.4 if you are moving fast. Damage per bullet 9. Reaction 0.45 s.\n\n**Vehicles:** patrol technicals follow road polylines with a PD steering controller, chase if alert, and their crews dismount at 25 m.\n\n**Barks** (gibberish voice plus a short subtitle, spatial): at least 15 per faction: spotted, lost you, grenade, jammed, fire, ally down. Examples in section 15.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Vehicles\n\nThree vehicles on `Vehicle` (tuning in section 3; truck heavier and slower): **pickup**, **technical** (pickup with MG on a pintle), **cargo truck** (also a tanker variant for M3). All have `model()` bodies, four wheel meshes posed by `wheelPose`, and suspension from the engine. One train: a kinematic body moved along the railway polyline, with flatcars.\n\n**Driving in first person:** eye at the driver's seat, your hands on a wheel that turns with steering, a dash, a small arrow on the dash pointing to your waypoint. R3/C toggles a chase camera (computed in your `step`, via `cam.override`). You can raise the map while driving, as you should: it's the most famous moment in this kind of game, and it's dangerous.\n\n**Getting in and out:** Use at a door. The player's capsule is disabled while seated and placed by the door on exit. Triangle/F swaps to the technical's gun; the truck rolls on with no driver, and that is your problem.\n\n**Damage:** 100 HP, from bullets, rockets, fire and collisions (over 8 m/s). Below 35: smoke, power × 0.6. Below 10: flames, explodes in 8 s unless you bail. At 0 and not on fire: stalled. **Repair:** stand at the bonnet, hold Use 3 s (wrench animation with sparks), back to 50 HP. The fixed truck coughs for a few seconds. Vehicles in M1 are scripted to break so everyone learns it.\n\nParking at a safehouse repairs fully overnight.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Buddies\n\nThree hired friends, each a named person with a real arc. All live in the bar at Mile Forty or at safehouses once you've met them.\n\n| | Ines Carvalho | Dov Halpern | Mags Oyelaran |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Met | Freed from the cage, M2 | Tibor's shed, after M2; his plan in M4 | Clinic, after your first Mercy Run |\n| Look | Flight cap, sunglasses, sand jacket | Bald, bandoliers, bad left hand | Braids, olive vest, medic's satchel with green cross |\n| Perk | Rescues you by truck, from farther away | Gives 2 free charges per mission | +1 syrette at any safehouse; rescues you with 60 HP instead of 40 |\n| Offers the alternate plan in | M3 | M4 | M5 |\n| Voice | Mid, quick | Low, slow | High, warm, never stops |\n\n**Alternate plans.** On certain contracts the buddy calls (radio squelch, subtitles) with a better way, adding objectives and more risk, in exchange for bonus stones and +1 trust. You can accept or ignore them on the spot. If you take it, they fight beside you on that mission.\n\n**Rescue.** At HP 0 with a buddy available: the screen drops, and you are dragged backward in first person (low camera, sky, their boots, their gunfire) while they swear at you; then you are up at 40 HP (Mags 60) with them next to you, and the buddy is spent for 1 game day. 15% chance they take a wound and are out for 2 days. They remind you of it later.\n\n**Down.** A buddy fighting with you has 200 HP. At 0 they fall, wounded, for 40 s, marked by a floating pixel cross. At their side, a two-button prompt:\n- `[Square/E] SYRETTE` (uses one; they're up at 50%, trust +1)\n- `[Triangle/F] FINISH IT` (one shot, your sidearm's animation)\n\nIf you have no syrettes, only the second option is lit. If the 40 s run out, they die anyway. If they are burning, they ask you to finish it. M6 forces this moment at least once.\n\nA dead buddy stays dead: name scratched on the safehouse wall, their gun leaning under it, one Ledger line, and no more of their lines anywhere. The game must play on without any of them. Write their missions so the alternate plan just doesn't arrive.\n\n**At the end,** every surviving buddy comes to the Pass for the stones (section 14). Those with trust 3 or more first offer to split it, then draw anyway if you refuse.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Stones, safehouses, side content\n\n**Rough stones** are the money.\n- Missions pay 5 to 25; cases hold 2 to 6.\n- Total available in the demo is about 180; everything for sale costs about 130.\n- Spent at **Tibor's** (a gun rack and a table).\n\n**Tibor's stock:** the guns in section 8 (buying one unlocks clean copies at every locker for free, forever), bottles, charges, and four upgrades:\n- Cleaning kit (6 stones): wear × 0.6.\n- Syrette pouch (5): carry 5.\n- Ammo belt (5): +50% reserve.\n- Vehicle kit (6): repair in 1.5 s.\n\n**The tracker.** Given in M4. D-pad Left/T raises it in your left hand: a small device with an LED and a beep that speeds up and turns green within 60 m of a stone case. Twelve cases are hidden around the Kettle (wrecks, hollow trees, under a dead man's truck, in guarded sheds).\n\n**Safehouses** (3):\n- **Bed:** sleep to Dawn, Noon, Dusk or Midnight, and it saves.\n- **Locker:** set your loadout.\n- **Crate:** ammo and syrettes.\n- A wall for names. Clear the guards first where there are any. Buddies wait here with offers.\n\n**Rail halts** (4): fast travel once visited, 1 stone, 1 game hour. It costs you Old Bakr's latest thought on a black screen with clacking wheels.\n\n**Trig points** (6): stone pillars on hilltops. Use one: a new note in Harrow's hand appears in your map's margin, and you can read it on the map. All six: he leaves you a clean `empire` at a locker. A note that says so.\n\n**Mercy Runs** (3, from Odile, plus M1): carry a crate to a marked place within 5 minutes. Pays 4 tablets. Fail and she says nothing, which is worse.\n\n**The Ledger.** Create/J in play, also in the pause menu, and at the end. One sheet, five big lines, at most (never a table):\n- Hectares burned (burnt cells × 0.0025)\n- People moved off their land\n- Men you were paid to kill\n- Friends lost\n- Stones earned\n\nWritten as Harrow would write it.\n\n**Saving:** single slot, versioned JSON in `localStorage` (`src/core/save.js`), written on sleep and on mission complete, read through `ctx.input.send` so replays stay exact. Main menu's Continue loads it. Dev mode has Quick Save/Load.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Missions\n\n**Format** (`src/content/missions.js`), a sketch:\n```js\n{ id:'m3', title:'FUEL FOR THE PEACE', giver:'vance', requires:['m2'], pay:15, wind:{ dir:[-0.8,-0.6], speed:5 },\n  brief:['vance:m3.brief.1','vance:m3.brief.2'],\n  alt:{ buddy:'ines', call:'ines:m3.alt', bonus:5, steps:[ {do:'steal',tag:'tanker',text:'TAKE THE TANKER AT P3'} ] },\n  steps:[ {do:'goto',site:'east-siding',r:140,text:'REACH THE SIDING'},\n          {do:'destroy',tag:'fuel-tank',n:3,text:'BURN THE TANKS'},\n          {do:'leave',site:'east-siding',r:220,text:'GET OUT'},","numLines":250,"startLine":350,"totalLines":1129}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0126fXkoUWdL1vQrokXo34FJ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
